## 3. Referenced at runtime by reflection — no code adapted

### 3.1 RTSCamera / MissionLibrary

Source: <https://github.com/lzh-mb-mod/RTSCamera> — MIT, Copyright (c) 2020 Li Zhenhuan

**No RTSCamera code was copied into this project.** The project's own record
(`tools/bl_rts.py`, `src/CameraFollow.cs`, `src/CameraSpeed.cs`) states the policy as
"we only act as a parameter administrator for it; we do not touch its code".

Verified against the actual source tree:

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| `using MissionSharedLibrary…` / `using RTSCamera…` (compile-time reference) | **none** |
| `GetMethod(` / `Invoke(` / `GetProperty(` (runtime reflection) | used in `CameraFollow.cs`, `CameraSpeed.cs` |
| Any RTSCamera implementation body reproduced here | **none** |

What the project actually does:

1. `tools/bl_rts.py` reads and writes RTSCamera's **configuration file**
   (`Configs/RTSCamera/RTSCameraConfig.xml`) only.
2. `src/CameraFollow.cs` / `src/CameraSpeed.cs` locate RTSCamera's **public API**
   (`MissionSharedLibrary.Utilities.Utility.BeforeSetMainAgent` / `AfterSetMainAgent`,
   `MissionLibrary.Controller.Camera.ACameraControllerManager`) by name and call it via
   reflection. This is deliberate: a compile-time reference would make BlBridge fail to
   load when RTSCamera is absent.

Upstream documentation (its own source and decompiled local copy) was **read to learn the
public API contract and the required call order** — that is interoperability, not copying.
The upstream MIT notice is recorded here **for transparency**, even though no RTSCamera
code is distributed with this project.

